{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/wraith-the-oblivion/16755-was-wraith-s-setting-too-punishing","id":16755,"title":"Was Wraith's setting too punishing?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Wraith: The Oblivion"],"posts":[{"date":"2013-11-23T13:22:09-08:00","id":16755,"title":"Was Wraith's setting too punishing?","content":"While doing some googling on Wraith, I came across this rather... depressing review of the game. The overall point of the review is that Wraith's setting is much, much more punishing than the premise (being a ghost) implies. While the premise implies something not unlike Patrick Swayze's <i>Ghost</i>, the actual setting turns up the bleakness to Warhammer 40K levels, except without the over-the-top awesomeness. I've cross-referenced this with the wiki and what books I could get and the assessment seems to be accurate, but the books really don't pull together the sheer bleakness implied as well as this review does. Is Wraith really much more bleaker than it has to be?<br /> <br /> EDIT: I've removed the attachment and just will post the review here:<br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Chris De Lasombra</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">For reference I would like to let you know right off how much I am devoted to WW and both the New and Classic WoD. I own all but 6 books from the CWoD and they are just reprints of stuff I already own and almost all of the NwoD, but it has been a bit more difficult to keep up with what is available since the distribution model has changed and I am horrible about keeping on top of it without my game store calling me to tell me my new book is in. What I am attempting to make relevant here is that I have supported WW WoD since I preordered the Vampire the Masquerade First Edition in 1991 and have no plans on not doing so even if I am not completely ecstatic about a game line they put out. No matter my complaints I am still addicted.<br /> <br /> Now on to my comments. Personally I thought the powers kind of sucked and I have to disagree with you about combat being better than any of the other games (at least that is how I read the intent of your statement). Sure, over all there were a couple nifty Arcanoi powers, but as a whole, they just did not add up to the cool factor like Mage or Vampire in my opinion. I liked them about as much as the Changeling powers.<br /> <br /> There are a couple of things that I disliked about Wraith, though I was absolutely enthralled with the concept of the game as a whole and obviously liked it enough to buy all the books. I still like reading the material, there are some really great things about the game and world that I find more than interesting, but as things stand I would rather read about it than try to play or ST it to any extent. For me it is similar to the likes of many of the Monte Python or Mel Brooks movies. They are much more funny to talk about and remissness than try to watch any more. The material is great, but I ran far too long on nostalgia and became disappointed because of the expectations I had and was no longer provided<br /> <br /> After reading Wraith Second Ed, absorbing the material and finally discerning how it all fit together, what I saw was a mess, not that First Ed was any better. More on that later. Definitely not the type of tragic mess and laughable disappointment I encountered by glancing through the D20 supplement, .The Book of Erotic Fantasy.. Oh, the tragedy that was .The Book of Erotic Fantasy.. Marlin Brando put it best I think. .The Horror..... but there was a melancholy disappointment that to this day I can not shake.<br /> <br /> Anyway, after I pieced together the .Big Picture. it looked to me that Danskey took a lot of really great ideas, both on his own and transfers from Fist Edition and threw them in a blender full of syrup, extracted the jumble, tied some of the pieces together with old frayed and molding bailing string, stuck some bubble gum for some color and stapled the rest with rusty staples pulled from his ninth grade homework. There were aspects of both Editions that I preferred over the other, but over all Second Edition was better.<br /> <br /> Allegory to this, I experienced the same thing when I read Hunter the Reckoning once I reached the rules section. What the hell is wrong with these people and why the hell did they find it necessary to change the formula that they had used successfully with every other game in the line? Hell, as different as they where, even Aeon, Exalted and Scion only made tweaks to an already solid and wildly popular system. Sure they all have/had their problems, but I challenge anyone to name a system that does not in one form or another. I had given up on the system they mistakenly (again, my own opinion) wrote for whatever reason for the Hunter Game and decided to just rewrite it to what I was familiar with and it ended up being pretty damned easy. It has been working just fine and my players are happier for it, especially those that were familiar with the Hunter system. After reading the Edges section a second (possibly a third) time and trying to figure out what the hell the host of authors were trying to accomplish since the paths made absolutely no sense in the order they were printed. What I noticed was if the individual powers were broken down and rearranged in a particular order, they actually would easily form paths of progressions that had a cohesive and understandable theme that I, and surely everyone else that plays or runs in the WoD would be much more familiar with. A few of the Edges did need to slide up or down on the scale from where they originated to fit where they were needed, but let's be honest, Hunter Edges were grossly overpowered for the level they were, but it is true that they only progressed up to level five so once I found that out I was not as against it. Ultimately moving a few few of them up or down had little effect on game play from what I could tell.<br /> <br /> Back to Wraith. The first problem I still have with Wraith is the structure of the society and by extension the Dead Lands as a whole. The second is that over half of the Arcanoi are not only illegal to use, but you can be taken away for simply being able to use them and there is literally no way to hide the telltale proof that everyone knows about.<br /> <br /> So lets start with the .citizens. of whatever Necropolis you may have happened upon, miraculously surviving till you actually got there or you were ridiculously lucky and were Cauled in a .civilized. Necropolis and the reaper that freed you did not immediately sell you into slavery or as scrap metal for some amazingly unknown and extremely unlikely reason. The citizenry Wraith that have .lived. in this Necropolis long before your freshly squeezed ass showed up likely see you as nothing more than a tool, future slave, their new footstool or someone to throw to the wolves so they can escape. Long story short, by some grand miracle you were able to last long enough to avoid any number of horrible fates and acquire an adequate leverage of some sort to keep the Slavers and the local Constabulary from just breaking down your door (if you even have one since materials are scarce as all hell) and executing any number of the exciting, unspeakable things that could easily happen to your character that you have likely not even spent a single XP on. In all reality, this entire time your character, likely with absolutely no helpful information to work with, has to try to figure out what the fuck is going on, how to survive, how to feed and where you can find a safe place to hide where all the sheltered space is already claimed. All this while trying to fend off the Wraiths so you are not hauled off by any number of people or even sold in the middle of the .night. by the same wraiths neighbors that you have to coexist with or face the unknown wild, the loneliness that will inevitably feed your Shadow and access to all the Harrowing's you could ever wish for.<br /> <br /> So here are all these wraiths around you that have been around an unknowable amount of time, know more than you do and would likely sell their own mother just to avoid their inevitable fate, living under the same cobbled together ghetto with an overabundance of other lying, back stabbing yet understandably paranoid pieces of shit that will gleefully throw you under the proverbial bus in the blink of an eye if they thought it would get them favor with whoever the local Wraith in charge is for even one more day of freedom. It turns out that fear is a great motivator to lead you to do things you would not not normally do<br /> <br /> Even though the Hierarchy claims to have law and order they are obviously nothing more than a bunch of bullies that prey on anyone weaker than they are and utilize those willing to do anything to do their dirty work and gleefully hand suffering out to everyone else around them with absolutely no consequence for their actions. Actually they are rewarded for their extremely evil behavior since in reality the Dead Lands are nothing more than a mashed together cacophony of barely controlled chaos stemmed from the stumbling attempts at fascist control through fear and intimidation and the resulting anarchy that it produces as a byproduct from the Renegades. No, the Renegades are not your saving grace either. They are not your friends, and like everything and everyone I have talked about before, it is unlikely any of them would come to your rescue even if you were being raped by a thorn dicked, venomous velociraptor that spits acid and three of his friends even if they could without any threat to themselves. The lot of them are not noble freedom fighter, they are brigands, thieves and murderers, not your friend.<br /> <br /> Which brings us to Charon, the totalitarian, fascist, dictator that supposedly rules over all of the Dead Lands by mere threat of being the monster under your bed that wants to consume your soul and there is no proof whether it is actually a monster or Charon since he has not been seen for so long nobody really remembers what he looks like. Not that a Wraith of his age and likely power would not possess the simple ability to change what he looks like on a whim anyway, so Charon could really be anyone, anywhere, at any time. There are of course the stories that cannot be proven false and actually because of Wraiths disappearing on a regular basis it is more likely that he is out there and actually does want to eat your soul because it is the only way he can continue existing. By now you may have realized you are slowly edging into doing things you never thought you would be capable of doing in a million years. It may even frighten you that you have to admit to yourself, you can't blame him for doing what he needs to stay alive even though it means your death and probably scares the shit out of you for more than one reason. The realization that there would be little to nothing you could do to escape him or fight him off if he did come for you anyway so really.....still just as fucked as you were to begin with. Eventually you will be caught in the wrong place at the wrong time and your bad day will happen. The possible horror filled ends you your first trip through the deadlands will likely be more violent than you imagined all the times you were forced to think about it. Likely it will be something that was human at one time much like yourself and it will rip your eyes out, replace them with coals of Bale Fire while making you watch with your own ripped out eyes then shove them up your ass so you are forced to watch yourself burn from the inside out. All because you finally gave in and trusted someone that turns out to have been taken over by the same darkness that you have struggled against and managed to keep suppressed.<br /> <br /> Oh and the ghetto you live in is still not safe by the way so you are still nagged by not only your thoughts, but your Shadow's as well. with escaping somehow before someone bigger and badder than you (still just Cauled remember) beats you into submission and sells you into slavery or worse, to an Artificer that will turn you into a broom, money a brick wall or a sex toy for his pet Loveland Frog. This of course should lead one to escaping into the ridiculously dangerous badlands with constant Wraith mauling Maelstroms that will shred you to pieces in less time than you could find unoccupied shelter. Which is unlikely because if you did find a .safe. place to get out of the storm, something else that is uniformly bigger and more bad ass than your still newly Cauled ass would never let a prime real estate location like that go used let alone unattended. Just for the sake of argument and whimsical fancy, say you did make it that far and actually avoided getting too near a Nihil, or one that just happens to open up directly beneath you, just because they do that sort of thing. All you have to look forward to is a lonely existence with your own wretched hate filled and manipulative Shadow slowly eating away at your sanity until it completely turns you into an evil life hating POS. Your list of options disappearing quicker than a dollar bill between a strippers ass cheeks, you somehow, by the grace of the god (the same one that probably made this place, or in the very least let it happen by the way), find a Pardoner. Not that you would know what that is or what they do, but what do you think the odds are she would actually help someone she does not know that can't offer her anything worth a rabid goats afterbirth in trade except going back to the previous problem of slavery and/or being turned into a Shadowland monsters favorite sex toy, when her own Guild and the service she can provide would get her ass in serious trouble with the Constabulary just by admitting she can do it. You think she would chance that? I am damn sure she would not in the cutthroat environment the world of Wraith provides.<br /> <br /> For entertainment sake lets just say there is the strikingly unlikelihood that you could find a Renegade that will not add you to his of soulsteal forged My Little Pony collection, and considers to let you join them. Again, you still have absolutely nothing to bargain with, or offer, not even a stitch of fucking clothing on your back in some cases. You are more likely to get thrown into a Nihil by Gunnery Sergeant Hartman while informing you not only are you a queer, but a steer to boot. One way or another you end up Harrowing and it is more than fair to say that a character surviving this long under those circumstances would be a considered a miracle. That is when you discover that you just to come back and have the pleasure of doing it all over again only a little quicker this time because your Shadow just gained itself a little more power.<br /> <br /> That all said, I do like me some horror, personal or otherwise, but even I have my limits. This would be more like being subjected to a 168 hour nonstop marathon of looped back to back screenings of .Requiem for a Dream., .The Road. and .The Human Centipede. sprinkled with Justin Bieber videos and interviews. But mostly it is the split in Arcanoi you have to deal with as a player that I really don't like. And not many of them are all that special or cool enough that I would want to play because the super powers are fun to use and experiment with. Essentially the vast separation of Arcanoi between what is useful in the Dead Lands and what is useful in the Prim Material have a very obvious line that makes a campaign set solely in the Dead Lands a lot less exciting when any concept I can think of for said campaign could be applied to another game that has better options for useful super powers. Quiet honestly, I play games to personalize something that I am not capable of doing and using my imagination and puzzle solving abilities to solve presented problems utilizing those powers to their fullest advantage. With Wraith it just feels too striped down and too often not accessible.</div> </div> </div> </div>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1021,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1021-raneth","user":"Raneth","date":". 11-23-2013, 05:01 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1021-raneth","name":"Raneth","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"58"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-11-23T13:42:15-08:00","id":16768,"title":"","content":"If you want something more like <i>Ghost, </i>Wraith is too bleak. But Wraith never sells itself as that. I don't think the premise of the game implies anything all that light-hearted. Ghost stories are generally bleak ones.<br /> <br /> I also don't see how Wraith gets to WH40K levels of bleakness. Wraith could, theoretically, get better. There is hope and the possibility of transcending. There is the ability to make a difference in the world; even if small and personally ones.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-11-23T13:59:11-08:00","id":16777,"title":"","content":"EDIT: I've appended the review to my OP since it is easy to miss.<br /> <br /> I\u2019m not talking about the being dead part or even the Shadow (both of those things are manageable despite the bad rap), but the fact that the people and institutions look like they came from Warhammer 40K. Pretty much everyone will try to enslave, sell and soulforge you at the first opportunity, you can be executed for simply knowing half the arcanoi in the game (and you carry obvious tell-tales), and pretty much \u201cyour afterlifespan is measured in minutes.\u201d Assuming the troupe ignores or severely downplays that, there\u2019s a sharp divide in the game\u2019s already indistinct focus: is the game about a ghost dealing with the living, or about a ghost dealing with the bizarre afterlife? There\u2019s no real connection between the two and you will be executed for trying to associate with the living.<br /> <br /> The game has a very, very good idea at its core, but the execution is far too extreme and loses sight of the original point. You\u2019re dead, so obviously trying to deal with the living with be fraught will problems, but it\u2019s absurdly cruel to create a horrible, malevolent afterlife society that actively discourages you from pursuing your unfinished business.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1021,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1021-raneth","user":"Raneth","date":". 11-23-2013, 05:02 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1021-raneth","name":"Raneth","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"58"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-11-23T15:33:18-08:00","id":16826,"title":"","content":"I think the review and your interpretation of Wraith society is skewed. It seems to be vastly overstating certain elements (the soulforging based economy's inherent nastiness), or downplaying or outright ignoring things that make it functional (the Guilds and Legions needing new recruits to bolster their ranks so there's actually incentives to give the newly dead that seem to have a chance a place in society, being an evil schmuck is a quick ticket to Spectre-hood so any long existing wraith knows that promoting a functional society is in their best interest to keep Shadows at bay).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-11-23T16:39:02-08:00","id":16865,"title":"","content":"I don't think it was necessarily more &quot;punishing&quot; than other game settings. To paint with the broad brush of generalizations, <i>Vampire</i> says &quot;The Elders will always and constantly control you and/or the Antediluvians will eventually eat you;&quot; <i>Werewolf</i> was &quot;You cannot win, the Wyrm has already won, the War is lost, no matter how many small battles you win;&quot; same with <i>Mage</i>, except replace &quot;Wyrm&quot; with &quot;Technocracy;&quot; and <i>Changling</i> said &quot;Arcadia is lost, gone - creativity and imagination are dying, live inside this mortal shell that will eventually decay.&quot; I think the setting for <i>Wraith</i> is actually more realized, more developed, then other settings, and more of a sense of (continuous) history.<br /> <br /> Finally, Stygian Law only goes as far as Stygia can enforce it. There are several examples of Legionnaires turning their heads at infractions, being bribed, et cetera. The write-ups for Necropoli make sure to point out how much control Stygia <i>really</i> has in each area.<br /> <br /> Cheers!","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>If you don't use an Oxford comma, I feel bad for you, son,<br /> 'Cuz I got ninety-nine problems, but clarity ain't one.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/408-nothing","name":"nothing","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar408_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"572"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-11-23T21:09:09-08:00","id":16926,"title":"","content":"Eh, I never liked Stygian rule too much. I'm too much of a gdi. There's this remake called <a href=\"http://wraithproject.cattail.nu/archives/wraiththearising.html\" target=\"_blank\">Arising</a>. How's it compare to the classic Wraith?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1021,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1021-raneth","user":"Raneth","date":". 11-24-2013, 07:36 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1021-raneth","name":"Raneth","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"58"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-11-26T05:46:13-08:00","id":18368,"title":"","content":"I have to agree with that review. Although Wraith is supposed to be about hope, it was so Very very hard to see it. It's hell... a Hell of purgatory. Maybe not as bad as things could be for you on the other side, but it's not that great either. Charon did what he could. Had all the good intentions of making something out of the nothing they were given when they couldn't reach Paradise (I believe the lyrics of VNV Nation -Kingdom sum that up quite well.), but it's turned out for the worse and even he knew that. Really it came down to YOU'RE DEAD! THAT'S IT! So afterlife is just more confusion and struggle and pain until you're either captured, harvested and made into something else, become as wretched as those above you just to exist or you destroy yourself and give in to Oblivion. Where's the chance at Salvation? Redemption? Even Reincarnation? None of that is really an option unless you find- DUN DUN DUNNNN! -a Ferryman... and up until the last book we get very little idea what they are all about or what happens if we do follow their advice and grow and let go of all that we thought mattered and become unfettered. Course, if that's how you felt as one of the newly dead, then what the hell was all the angst and strife for? And Shadows? Christ on toast! Do we really need STs digging at us players constantly about our self doubts, our inadequacies, out faults and failures? Yeah they're just characters, but who wants to hear that kind of ridicule constantly in a game and call it canon? No Thank you.<br /> <br /> And there's those of us where Death and the question of the afterlife hits closer to home. Losing someone you care about and then having to focus on this game that is supposed to be a fun diversion, outline how futile and fragile existence is in the Shadowlands is just too much to ask. It's Bleak.... I'm not even sure anything about that can be changed and still have it be the same game. Oblivion is in the damn title even. There's nothing more despairing than facing the end of all things.<br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://imagizer.imageshack.us/v2/125x125q90/19/w20supporterheavymetal1.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://imagizer.imageshack.us/v2/125x125q90/31/25ew.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://imagizer.imageshack.us/v2/125x125q90/829/8seg.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><a href=\"http://glyphery.webs.com/\" target=\"_blank\"><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://i.imgur.com/Ut0SZ4h.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /></a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1332-darling_rose","name":"Darling_Rose","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1332_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"434"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-11-26T06:44:05-08:00","id":18380,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Losing someone you care about and then having to focus on this game that is supposed to be a fun diversion, outline how futile and fragile existence is in the Shadowlands is just too much to ask. </div> </div> </div>It's pretty self-evident that if you're in mourning for someone who has died then you probably aren't going to enjoy a horror game about ghosts.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://theonyxpath.com/downloads/Backer%20Badge%20small.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/178-michael","name":"Michael","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar178_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3560"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-11-26T15:52:38-08:00","id":18778,"title":"","content":"Yeah.... I have to agree with Michael that sounds a little too personal.<br /> <br /> Anyway you're forgetting about one important thing, something that sets WtO above all other CWoD games, Transcendence, resolving your Wraith's unfinished business so they can move on as they should have. Now that may not seem like much but compared to the other gamelines it's a definitive, achievable end goal. For instance in VtM no matter how much power you achieve there will always be a bigger, nastier and more powerful vampire out there who's probably pulling your strings.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://youtu.be/_yBS2EZFduI?t=2m13s\" target=\"_blank\">Homo sapiens. What an inventive, invincible species. It's only a few million years since they crawled up out of the mud and learned to walk. Puny, defenceless bipeds. They've survived flood, famine and plague. They've survived cosmic wars and holocausts. And now, here they are, out among the stars, waiting to begin a new life. Ready to outsit eternity. They're indomitable. Indomitable.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/420-akatsukileader13","name":"AkatsukiLeader13","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar420_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1637"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-11-26T18:11:20-08:00","id":18847,"title":"","content":"*nods* That was a reason I had to distance myself from that particular system for a while. Unlike all the other systems, Wraith deals with very personal themes. You can't distance yourself from it as much as Mage or Werewolf or Vampire if something bad happens in your life. (&quot;Werewolves ate my Grandmother? ARRG!&quot;)<br /> <br /> I do agree with that comparison about Vampire. However Wraith has so much going against you as a newbie from the start. You're not a neonate preying on humans and watching out for older Vampires. You don't start out at the top of the mortal food chain. In Wraith you are the lowest rung of everything and everything is trying to tear you apart or use you if you don't destroy yourself.<br /> <br /> There's gothic and then there's grim... There just isn't much room for trying to find Transcendance as a character or resolving your unfinished business if you're busy trying to hide from the Legion, the Renegades, the Hierarchy, your Shadow... let's just say hiding from Everyone except Ferrymen; ad tedium. It might actually be easier to manage if the Shadowlands actually was Hell in a classical sense. Very cut and dry Demons are the things to hide from, enlightened beings/transcended ghosts are the beings to seek for help and all the while you're trying to survive being torn apart by Maelstrom winds and surviving in pockets of other ghosts working together. Almost a Zombie Apocalypse sort of scenario in the underworld except you are already dead. Then at least there's some idea of refuge and trying to find your way instead of landing in a place where everything is out to get you and you're expected to find some sort of revelation. Has anyone reading here had a revelation of existence in the middle of playing Resident Evil? If you have, kudos! Tell us about it. Still, the chances seem overwhelmingly stacked against any Wraith character amounting to anything other than a pawn in a repetitive play of misery. Not much of a game. I think it still needs a lot of work to open it up a bit more for an enjoyable game. There's a lot of promise there.<br /> <br /> <br /> Say if they made it more apparent that the Far Shores didn't seem so far, there might be more incentive to struggle. When you can See the goal and dream of redemption, it makes the journey more worthwhile. You have a clear place you want to get to. For anyone who's read Sandman, Morpheus said it best &quot;What power would Hell have over its denizens if they could not dream of Heaven?&quot; With a clear and ever present goal to reach, the intensity of the game is higher and so are the stakes of existence in the Shadowlands.<br /> <br /> It's been stated that Werewolf is a pointless game because they've already lost the War to the Wyrm, or that Mages have already lost to the Technocracy. However in the cases of Werewolves, they have too much Rage and passion for their hopeless cause to just roll over and die. They'll go down fighting like the born warriors they are.There's always the chance they'll take all their enemies down with them in the end. Or at least sacrifice themselves to free the earth of all corruption. Still a hopeless cause but a very clear one. So lets talk Magi. As much as Technocracy has the upper hand now, it won't always be as static as they want. A world without magic is a poor substitute for a safe world. Humanity subconsciously craves wonder and mysticism. Once the stasis gets boring, the Magi will regroup like vermin in the walls and continue to work miracles until Humanity is ready to welcome believing in magic back into their lives. Same could be said for Changelings too. The same is true enough in our world, it can be true to a lesser extent in the World of Darkness.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://imagizer.imageshack.us/v2/125x125q90/19/w20supporterheavymetal1.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://imagizer.imageshack.us/v2/125x125q90/31/25ew.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://imagizer.imageshack.us/v2/125x125q90/829/8seg.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><a href=\"http://glyphery.webs.com/\" target=\"_blank\"><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://i.imgur.com/Ut0SZ4h.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /></a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1332-darling_rose","name":"Darling_Rose","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1332_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"434"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-11-26T19:31:58-08:00","id":18887,"title":"","content":"I don't get the repeated idea that every organization in the Underworld is out to destroy/enslave/etc. the newly dead. The Legion needs recruits. The Guilds need recruits. The Renegades need supporters and members. All of these organizations need a society that both enables and justifies their existence.<br /> <br /> Are some new wraiths screwed on waking up? Sure. Some vampires get killed for being embraced without permission. Some Garou get killed by Wyrm-servitors before the Nation can find them. Some mages can sent to room 101 before the Traditions can save them. So how is Wraith special by having a setup where there are people out to screw new supernaturals before they can even get their bearings?<br /> <br /> You don't play those. You play a wraith that managed to get past that initial hurdle and once past it, you have a lot of setting to interact with.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-11-26T20:33:13-08:00","id":18916,"title":"","content":"Indeed. This fixation on how some Wraith end up as Spectres, material for the Legions and Hierarchy or otherwise destroyed isn't really what it's like for plenty of Wraiths. In fact most of the souls that get sent right away to the forges are those souls most likely to quickly succumb to Spectredom, such as Drones, the Wraiths of children and clearly weak-willed. Do ordinary Wraiths get scooped up among them too, of course but as Heavy pointed out that isn't something exclusive to Wraith and the PCs are generally assumed to have gotten past those hurdles.<br /> <br /> Also the Shadowlands and the Underworld as a whole isn't Hell, or rather one of the Hells present in the CWoD. The Shadowlands are basically a ghostly reflection of the Skinlands, our world while the rest of the Underworld beneath it tends to be surreal and grim than a hellish landscape until you great to the Labyrinth but that's something else altogether different from Hell. Nor are their 'demons' or monsters or whatever wandering all over the place. In fact the Necropoli are about as safe as any mortal city in the WoD.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://youtu.be/_yBS2EZFduI?t=2m13s\" target=\"_blank\">Homo sapiens. What an inventive, invincible species. It's only a few million years since they crawled up out of the mud and learned to walk. Puny, defenceless bipeds. They've survived flood, famine and plague. They've survived cosmic wars and holocausts. And now, here they are, out among the stars, waiting to begin a new life. Ready to outsit eternity. They're indomitable. Indomitable.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/420-akatsukileader13","name":"AkatsukiLeader13","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar420_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1637"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-11-27T04:30:03-08:00","id":19085,"title":"","content":"urg, I wrote a big reply and in trying to wrangle quotes the page reloaded and I lost it. damn... TL;DR Heavy: yes all those other supernats get killed and you get to start over. Wraith you are dead, what's the point of start overs?<br /> <br /> AktasukiLeader13... (think I wanna call you Akat): Saying Wraith ain't so bad and doesn't always have bad stuff out to get you is not doing the game any favors. I do have all the books, this thread asks is it too punishing, I think yes it is. Calling some areas safe and that there's good guys out there to join up with, sounds a House-rule take on a setting that is painted very dark in great detail in every Wraith book clearly outlining that you're dead... and you thought things couldn't get any worse. And that's fine with me or would be if there was more to it than that.<br /> <br /> I still think the game needs a more obvious OUT for players to strive for. A holy grail to journey towards, not just figuring out whatever it all means and going POOF. Stick a light at the end of the tunnel. Sure make it horribly punishing, but make the Hope more apparent. If you dumb down the danger that lurks around every corner to be &quot;about as safe as any mortal city&quot; then it may as well be pointless. The game of Wraith shouldn't be played like SIMS version of the Underworld. Why subject players to more of the same human rat race?<br /> <br /> If this was the question developers had to wrestle with and couldn't figure out a way to resolve these issues and make the game popular, then I can see why it was binned and never Revised. They painted themselves into a black little corner.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://imagizer.imageshack.us/v2/125x125q90/19/w20supporterheavymetal1.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://imagizer.imageshack.us/v2/125x125q90/31/25ew.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://imagizer.imageshack.us/v2/125x125q90/829/8seg.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><a href=\"http://glyphery.webs.com/\" target=\"_blank\"><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://i.imgur.com/Ut0SZ4h.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /></a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1332-darling_rose","name":"Darling_Rose","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1332_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"434"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-11-27T05:50:39-08:00","id":19117,"title":"","content":"I'm not saying the Underworld isn't a bad place or that Wraiths don't have it bad, I'm saying they aren't as bad as you keep on implying. Half the time you make it sound like there are a hundred different horrors waiting to devour the Wraith the moment they step out of their haunts. As for the Legions and the Hierarchy, I never said they were good guys. The truth is in the WoD there is no 'good' group/organization out there, only varying shades of grey. But they way you keep depicting them, like the WtO line as a whole, is through a very grim and negative, and simplistic for that matter, light.<br /> <br /> Honestly it's reminding me of debates I've had in the past involving the Technocracy.<br /> <br /> There is a very obvious OUT that's tied to the very nature of what the Wraiths are, it's called Transcendence. Wraiths are created because the person who died had something strong holding them back from crossing over hence in order to escape their current situation they need to resolve their Passions. I mean how much more obvious can it get? You want to escape the Underworld? Resolve your Passions before it's too late.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://youtu.be/_yBS2EZFduI?t=2m13s\" target=\"_blank\">Homo sapiens. What an inventive, invincible species. It's only a few million years since they crawled up out of the mud and learned to walk. Puny, defenceless bipeds. They've survived flood, famine and plague. They've survived cosmic wars and holocausts. And now, here they are, out among the stars, waiting to begin a new life. Ready to outsit eternity. They're indomitable. Indomitable.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/420-akatsukileader13","name":"AkatsukiLeader13","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar420_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1637"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-11-27T08:02:42-08:00","id":19184,"title":"","content":"If it was so unplayable, they never would have released the game.<br /> <br /> The fiction contained in the books show characters getting by just fine without having to hide every second of the day. The example of play shows a Hierarchy and Renegade crossing paths, exchanging friendly words and going about their business without open conflict.<br /> <br /> Minor rant....<br /> <div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\">Yes, the Underworld is rough. <b>OF COURSE IT'S ROUGH!</b> So many people die expecting their reward and get <b>THAT!</b> <i>Of course they're harsh</i>. 100 years of un-living, risking un-life and dead limb crossing the Tempest, the occasional Spectre attack, and dealing with whatever bullshit you left behind is going to make you a little cranky.<br /> <br /> It's not touchy-feely, it's not nicey-nicey. It's fucking (un)life and it forces people to take a good hard look at who they are, what they would regret and what that tiny inner voice would say if it got a megaphone.<br /> <br /> If it seems harsh, it's because it's the most human character type.</div></div> </div> </div>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>- If you must be ridiculous, I must ridicule you.<br /> - Those that can give up essential liberties in exchange for temporary safety deserve neither liberty nor safety. -- Benjamin Franklin</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/375-papa-bear","name":"Papa Bear","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar375_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1920"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-11-27T09:20:05-08:00","id":19217,"title":"","content":"One little thing here I didn't have time for in my last post.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Darling_Rose</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/wraith-the-oblivion/16755-was-wraith-s-setting-too-punishing?p=19085#post19085\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">TL;DR Heavy: yes all those other supernats get killed and you get to start over. Wraith you are dead, what's the point of start overs?.</div> </div> </div> </div>By that logic what's the point of starting over, or for that matter playing, with new character for any WoD game? VtM:You'll still be dead and damned. WtA:Best outcome you die quickly in battle. MtAs:The Technocracy is winning while the Sleepers by and large don't want 'magic'. CtD: Banality is coming...","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://youtu.be/_yBS2EZFduI?t=2m13s\" target=\"_blank\">Homo sapiens. What an inventive, invincible species. It's only a few million years since they crawled up out of the mud and learned to walk. Puny, defenceless bipeds. They've survived flood, famine and plague. They've survived cosmic wars and holocausts. And now, here they are, out among the stars, waiting to begin a new life. Ready to outsit eternity. They're indomitable. Indomitable.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/420-akatsukileader13","name":"AkatsukiLeader13","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar420_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1637"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-11-28T02:46:03-08:00","id":19632,"title":"","content":"The point of playing is a clearly defined goal and a path towards it. Kill your Sire, Kill the Wyrm, Subvert the Technos, Foster (or Ravage) the Glamour. Wraith seems to be: Rot in purgatory till you figure out what the hell you're doing there. The theme seems overbearingly futile without a clearer goal other than Fake it till you Make it.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://imagizer.imageshack.us/v2/125x125q90/19/w20supporterheavymetal1.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://imagizer.imageshack.us/v2/125x125q90/31/25ew.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://imagizer.imageshack.us/v2/125x125q90/829/8seg.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><a href=\"http://glyphery.webs.com/\" target=\"_blank\"><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://i.imgur.com/Ut0SZ4h.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /></a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1332-darling_rose","name":"Darling_Rose","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1332_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"434"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-11-28T07:55:21-08:00","id":19735,"title":"","content":"1) Those are not a truly representative list of &quot;clearly defined goals&quot; in the various cWOD games.<br /> <br /> 2) &quot;Resolve your Fetters/Passions&quot; is equal to all of them in how clearly it is presented as a primary long term character goal to strive for.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-11-28T14:16:03-08:00","id":19896,"title":"","content":"Heavy Arms: Indeed on both accounts. Transcendence is connected to the mechanics of the wraiths. You can't get any more clearly defined than that.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://youtu.be/_yBS2EZFduI?t=2m13s\" target=\"_blank\">Homo sapiens. What an inventive, invincible species. It's only a few million years since they crawled up out of the mud and learned to walk. Puny, defenceless bipeds. They've survived flood, famine and plague. They've survived cosmic wars and holocausts. And now, here they are, out among the stars, waiting to begin a new life. Ready to outsit eternity. They're indomitable. Indomitable.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/420-akatsukileader13","name":"AkatsukiLeader13","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar420_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1637"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-11-30T02:32:26-08:00","id":20563,"title":"","content":"I don't see it quite the same way you do then.<br /> <br /> Seems the original poster has been banned. I guess we can't ask if we have answered his question or not.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://imagizer.imageshack.us/v2/125x125q90/19/w20supporterheavymetal1.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://imagizer.imageshack.us/v2/125x125q90/31/25ew.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://imagizer.imageshack.us/v2/125x125q90/829/8seg.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><a href=\"http://glyphery.webs.com/\" target=\"_blank\"><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://i.imgur.com/Ut0SZ4h.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /></a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1332-darling_rose","name":"Darling_Rose","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1332_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"434"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-11-30T16:01:47-08:00","id":20840,"title":"","content":"Banned doesn't mean permanently banned, so we might yet.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-12-02T12:06:36-08:00","id":21813,"title":"","content":"One thing that occurred to me is that if joy was something that could not be found in the shadowlands... that many wraiths could not exist. It is something that is in short supply, undoubtedly, but not unheard of.<br /> <br /> I was thinking about Wraith today when I heard the song &quot;Stayin' Alive&quot; and my mind was immediately was arrested by the image of a trio of chanteurs strutting down the street with a relic boom box and I had to laugh. I thought that this was completely counter to the themes of the game. <br /> <br /> But then... is it really?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1078-froggy711","name":"Froggy711","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1078_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"314"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-12-02T12:16:40-08:00","id":21821,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Froggy711</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/wraith-the-oblivion/16755-was-wraith-s-setting-too-punishing?p=21813#post21813\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I was thinking about Wraith today when I heard the song &quot;Stayin' Alive&quot; and my mind was immediately was arrested by the image of a trio of chanteurs strutting down the street with a relic boom box and I had to laugh. I thought that this was completely counter to the themes of the game.<br /> <br /> But then... is it really?</div> </div> </div> </div>Not if you ask me. The characters are dead, what else are they going to do? Sit around and mope? The entire story of Erik in the Wraith books is all about a guy who WAS going to sit around and mope, and decided not to.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>- If you must be ridiculous, I must ridicule you.<br /> - Those that can give up essential liberties in exchange for temporary safety deserve neither liberty nor safety. -- Benjamin Franklin</i>","edited":{"userid":375,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/375-papa-bear","user":"Papa Bear","date":". 12-03-2013, 10:47 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/375-papa-bear","name":"Papa Bear","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar375_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1920"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-12-02T18:06:24-08:00","id":22020,"title":"","content":"Indeed. Erik's story across the 2nd Edition is something I've always loved about WtO and honestly it's a great example of how a WtO chronicle could go. And that the final WtO book, Ends of the Empire ends with Erik finally meeting the girl he's been looking for since the 2nd Rulebook, against all the odds, in the face of the 6GM and all it's chaos, he finds her. If that isn't something hopeful and beautiful in the WoD and more specifically in WtO, I don't know what is. In fact I think that might just be the most positive ending of any of the closing books of the CWoD.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://youtu.be/_yBS2EZFduI?t=2m13s\" target=\"_blank\">Homo sapiens. What an inventive, invincible species. It's only a few million years since they crawled up out of the mud and learned to walk. Puny, defenceless bipeds. They've survived flood, famine and plague. They've survived cosmic wars and holocausts. And now, here they are, out among the stars, waiting to begin a new life. Ready to outsit eternity. They're indomitable. Indomitable.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/420-akatsukileader13","name":"AkatsukiLeader13","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar420_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1637"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-12-02T18:25:55-08:00","id":22040,"title":"","content":"That was a good way to close the metaplot. <br /> The Sig character finds his girlfriend, gets recruited by the Ferrymen, and don\u2019t the PCs wind up as the new Deathlords? <br /> And \u2013 icing on the cake - EoE had my all all-time favorite cover illustration of any WW book. Talk about Pathos....","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-size:8px\"><span style=\"font-family:comic sans ms\">&lt;signature&gt; something witty &lt;/signature&gt;</span></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/302-nazfool","name":"Nazfool","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar302_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"753"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-12-06T16:46:27-08:00","id":24743,"title":"","content":"You know, I always hear/heard the perspective that Wraith is too hard or too 40k or w/e. Are there shared themes? Sure. Every game that exists shares themes with something else.<br /> <br /> They set it up as an oppressive and difficult 'world' to be a part of. You should not really be playing people with major failure complexes in Wraith like you can in Werewolf, or Changeling, or Vampire, etc. You need to play someone who wants to survive on such a fundamental level that they will go beyond death to continue. <br /> <br /> Maybe I'm wrong, but it just always feels like the arguments I've seen about one game being harder than another.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1559-cryocube","name":"cryocube","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1559_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"20"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-12-06T19:26:15-08:00","id":24844,"title":"","content":"I've always seen Wraith as a giant metaphor for the struggle against depression, so an oppressively shitty setting just serves to further the point. You have to find your happiness.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Remi. she/her. game designer.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/281-atamajakki","name":"atamajakki","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar281_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5487"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-12-07T12:31:43-08:00","id":25295,"title":"","content":"Actually, all of the World of Darkness games are written as 'dark.' It's in the name for crying out loud. Vampire is a game of gothic horror where you are dragged into the night as an immortal blood sucking monster, being manipulated by shadowy elders while your humanity erodes. In Werewolf, you rage against a dying world, likely dying a tragic (but hopefully heroic) death in battle. Both Mage and Changeling were urban fantasy, exploring the death of creativity, free will and even passion against a bleak, uncaring world. It's the whole 'goth-punk' thing that ran throughout all of the classic WW games. Wraith was a product of its time. Look back at the media of the 90s and you'll see a lot of bleak, oppressive imagery popping up in movies, comics, even music. WW was definitely part of that trend (and latched onto it for the WoD games).<br /> <br /> Yes, the default setting is not Ghost... or Caspar the Friendly Ghost, A Christmas Carol, or even Ghostbusters. But nothing says you CAN'T run it that way either, or at least mine those sources for inspiration. I would point out, of course, that one common conceit in all of the WoD games (and most RPGs actually) is that the PCs are unique, they are special. They have the will to stand out in their world. They very well may learn 'forbidden' Arcanoi; it wouldn't be in the core books and player's guide otherwise.<br /> <br /> Also, as presented, the authorities ARE very much the 'bad guys.' Stygia is a mess. It's corrupt, dangerous and oppressive. There might be good Legionnaires, or at least officials that can be bribed to look the other way, but as a whole the institutions are portrayed as very negative. Again, that's more a product of the times than anything. WoD games have tended to be rather anti-authoritarian, and this was especially pronounced in the 90s.<br /> <br /> I'd also point out later books were rather open about the fact that the Guilds still existed and were active in Stygian, some more or less tacitly accepted by the Hierarchy because their Arcanoi were just so useful. Others were mostly ignored even if they were engaged in illicit activities, either because they had paid off the right people, or because the Hierarchy had bigger fish to fry. And a few like the Haunters and Spooks were blatantly illegal but also too difficult to destroy outright.<br /> <br /> I think the biggest problem wasn't the dark, depressing 'goth-punk' atmosphere, which all of the oWoD game lines shared, but rather the fact that Wraith diverged from the standard 'our world, only darker' setting. Wraith may have been set in our world, but most of the action took place in a dark underworld with no real correlation to real world locations. It was essentially more dark fantasy, with a huge but vaguely defined cosmology.<br /> <br /> Yes, Werewolf and Mage had the Umbra, but that was more the reverse. The main setting was the physical world, with characters having to use powers to travel to the Umbra. In Wraith, you could indeed interact with the physical world. In fact, there were no shortage of powers specifically to do so... Pandemonium, Puppetry, Embody, even Inhabit! But it wasn't inherent; it requires special powers and sometimes effort on the part of the PCs.<br /> <br /> Also, perhaps as a consequence of being a relative latecomer, Wraith's cosmology never got fleshed out as fully as Werewolf's or Mage's. Or even Changeling's really. We got tons of books detailing the various Umbral Realms and their inhabitants, but Wraith only really got The Sea of Shadows, Dark Kingdom of Jade, and a few paragraphs scattered here and there through various supplements. I'm hoping that will be remedied in Wr20.<br /> <br /> Anyway, I'm not disparaging Wraith. I love the game, and honestly have found it more interesting as I've gone back and re-read the books. But I do think there are reasons some players (and STs) have a hard time 'getting into' the setting. Hopefully more of them will opt to play the game and find out for themselves instead of buying into the rumors that it was 'too dark.'","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1449,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1449-zakariya-ali-sher","user":"Zakariya Ali Sher","date":".  For some reason, my iPad didn't want to break that into paragraphs. Sorry 12-07-2013, 06:39 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1449-zakariya-ali-sher","name":"Zakariya Ali Sher","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"59"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}